From: winit patch <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] windows: cancel IME composition when IME is disallowed

When an application moves text focus from field A to field B it calls
`Window::set_ime_allowed(false)` and later `set_ime_allowed(true)`.
`set_ime_allowed` only flips `ime_allowed` and calls `ImmAssociateContextEx`;
it never resets `ime_state`. A `WM_IME_COMPOSITION` / `WM_IME_ENDCOMPOSITION`
that belongs to A and is dispatched after the switch is therefore handled with
`ime_allowed == true` (B's permission) and is reported as B's
`Ime::Preedit` / `Ime::Commit` / `Ime::Disabled`. The event vocabulary has no
field generation, so the application cannot tell the difference.

Reproduction: start a non-empty preedit in A, move focus to B immediately
(for example with Tab) and watch the `Ime` events B receives.

Fix:

- `set_ime_allowed(false)` cancels the composition in progress with
  `ImmNotifyIME(NI_COMPOSITIONSTR, CPS_CANCEL)` while `ime_allowed` is still
  true, so A's `Preedit("")` / `Disabled` are emitted at the moment of the
  blur request. No lock is held across the call because it re-enters the
  window procedure.
- `ime_state` is then reset to `Disabled`, so anything still queued for A is
  dropped by the existing `ime_allowed && ime_state != Disabled` check in
  `WM_IME_COMPOSITION`.
- `WM_IME_ENDCOMPOSITION` no longer emits `Ime::Disabled` when `ime_state`
  is already `Disabled`, so a late END of A cannot produce a spurious
  `Disabled` inside B's session.

Trade-offs:

- The uncommitted preedit of A is discarded (same as macOS). Committed text
  is not lost. `CPS_COMPLETE` (commit A's preedit) is an alternative.
- Comparing `HIMC` to detect the generation does not work: the same hwnd is
  re-associated with its default `HIMC` on every enable, so A and B share it.
- The END guard interacts with the Hangul IME, which sends
  `WM_IME_COMPOSITION` after `WM_IME_ENDCOMPOSITION`; that path still works
  because `ime_state` is only `Disabled` once END was handled.
---
